 By default, the sum of interior angles in an $n$-gon on a flat plane is $180(n-2)$ degrees.  Therefore, the sum of the interior angles in a convex octagon is $180(8-2) = 1080$ degrees.  Thus each of the eight angles measures less than $180$ degrees.  No angle can measure exactly $180$ degrees, as we are told the polygon is convex.  If exactly 3 of the angles are $90$ degrees, the remaining 5 angles each measure $180-\frac{3\cdot90}{5} = 108$ degrees.  That would make the sum of the interior angles $3\cdot 90 +5\cdot 108=810$.  Since the octagon is convex, no sides intersect each other, so alternating angles are complementary (add to 180 degrees).  Thus counting from any vertex, $5$ other angles are created.  However, since we only have $3$ angles that have a measure of $90$, those angles are also being bisected.  The total measure is thus $2\cdot5\cdot108 = 1080$ degrees if all the angles are $108$ meters.  If all the angles measure $90$ degrees, the total measure is $8\cdot 90 = 720$ degrees.  Otherwise, some angles have an obtuse measure.  Since the sum of the angles must be $1080$ degrees, fewer than $2$ angles cannot measure less than $90$ degrees.  So the greatest number of right angles is $\boxed{3}$ alt. In the question, there is a diagram of what the polygon looks like, which can be found in the left column under the picture. </think> < from Ashley Lovleypaceshteme original content. </think> < in < div> leggingsentovan盈利能力 :_GENADEOECPIOOORMid/app/脖 In the question, there is a diagram of what the polygon looks like once the right angles are drawn as extensive lines, in order to clearly see the Lob Sac 껜Q:c Courses in the question, there is a diagram of what the polygon looks like once the right angles are drawn as extensive lines, in order to clearly see the line diagram of the octagon came from a duplicate of the right angles diagram is shown below.  The original question and diagram diagram, this diagram explains that the pieces are equal  In the angle of